rel and visits the squirrel, then the squirrel doesn't see the lion. But the rabbit doesn't visit the squirrel; the squirrel visits the rabbit. So, the condition isn't met.\n\nTherefore, the only conclusion is that the rabbit does not see the lion because it's not big. So, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the user thought it was B) False. Maybe I'm wrong. Let me think differently.\n\nPerhaps the rabbit does see the lion through another route. Let me see.\n\nStatement 17: If X sees the lion and doesn't see the bear, then X sees the squirrel. The rabbit sees the bear, so if the rabbit saw the lion, it wouldn't have to see the squirrel. But the rabbit doesn't see the lion because it's not big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit sees the lion through another implication. Let me check statement 14 again.\n\nStatement 14: If X likes the squirrel, then the squirrel visits X. The rabbit likes the squirrel, so the squirrel visits the rabbit. So, the squirrel is at the rabbit's place, meaning the rabbit sees the squirrel.\n\nNow, statement 15: If X sees the squirrel and visits the squirrel, then the squirrel doesn't see the lion. The rabbit sees the squirrel (since the squirrel is visiting) and if the rabbit visits the squirrel, then the squirrel doesn't see the lion. But the rabbit doesn't visit the squirrel; the squirrel visits the rabbit. So, the condition isn't met.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel sees the lion because it visits the lion (statement 11). So, the squirrel sees the lion, which means the squirrel doesn't like the lion (statement 13).\n\nBut the rabbit doesn't see the lion because it's not big. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the user thought it was B) False. Maybe I'm missing something. Let me check all statements again.\n\nWait, statement 18: If X visits the rabbit, then X sees the lion. The bear visits the rabbit, so the bear sees the lion. The bear is big, so that's consistent.\n\nStatement 19: If X sees the lion, then X is big. So, the rabbit is not big, so it doesn't see the lion.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The rabbit does not see the lion\" is true. So, the correct answer is A) True.\n\nBut the user thought it was B) False. Maybe the user made a mistake. I think the correct answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Maybe the rabbit does see the lion through another implication.\n\nStatement 17: If X sees the lion and doesn't see the bear, then X sees the squirrel. The rabbit sees the bear, so if the rabbit saw the lion, it wouldn't have to see the squirrel. But the rabbit doesn't see the lion because it's not big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit sees the lion through statement 18. Wait, statement 18 says if X visits the rabbit, then X sees the lion. The bear visits the rabbit, so the bear sees the lion. But that doesn't mean the rabbit sees the lion.\n\nTherefore, the rabbit does not see the lion because it's not big. So, the statement is true.\n\nI think the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe rabbit does not see the lion because if it did, it would have to be big, which it is not. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
