hrough statement 19.\n\nWait, if someone visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel. So, if the bald eagle is visited by someone, it will visit the squirrel. But does the bald eagle get visited by anyone?.\n\nLooking at the statements, the mouse does not visit the bald eagle (statement 10), the squirrel does not visit the lion (statement 14), but does the squirrel visit the bald eagle? I don\'t see any statement about that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion visits the bald eagle, but as we saw earlier, that\'s not stated.\n\nWait, perhaps the squirrel visits the bald eagle? But statement 14 says the squirrel does not visit the lion, but nothing about visiting the bald eagle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse visits the bald eagle, but statement 10 says it doesn\'t.\n\nSo, perhaps no one visits the bald eagle. Therefore, statement 19 doesn\'t apply because the condition (something visits the bald eagle) isn\'t met. So, the conclusion (the bald eagle visits the squirrel) doesn\'t necessarily follow.\n\nBut that doesn\'t directly help with the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nLet me look for other statements that might relate to the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nStatement 20: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young. The squirrel is red, and the lion visits the squirrel, so the lion is young.\n\nStatement 18: If something is young, it visits the bald eagle. So, the lion is young, so it must visit the bald eagle. But the context doesn\'t say that, so perhaps the context is incomplete, but in the given context, the lion doesn\'t visit the bald eagle, which would mean the lion isn\'t young, contradicting statement 20.\n\nThis is a problem. Maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lion does visit the bald eagle, but it\'s not explicitly stated. So, perhaps the lion visits the bald eagle, which would satisfy statement 18, and then the bald eagle would visit the squirrel (statement 19). But the context doesn\'t mention the bald eagle visiting the squirrel, only chasing it.\n\nWait, but the bald eagle chases the squirrel (statement 2), which is a different action than visiting. So, perhaps the bald eagle doesn\'t visit the squirrel, which would mean that the condition in statement 19 isn\'t met, so the conclusion doesn\'t apply.\n\nBut this is getting complicated. Let me try to approach it differently.\n\nI need to determine if the bald eagle is young. So, I need to see if any statement implies that the bald eagle is young.\n\nLooking at statement 18: If something is young, it visits the bald eagle. So, if the bald eagle is young, then it would visit itself, which doesn\'t make sense. So, perhaps the statement is about others visiting the bald eagle if they are young.\n\nBut the question is about the bald eagle\'s age, not others\'. So, perhaps I need to find a statement that links the bald eagle\'s age to something else.\n\nLooking at statement 19: If something visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel. So, if someone visits the bald eagle, the bald eagle will visit the squirrel. But does the bald eagle visit the squirrel? The context says the bald eagle chases the squirrel (statement 2), but not that it visits.\n\nSo, unless someone visits the bald eagle, the bald eagle doesn\'t necessarily visit the squirrel. But since no one visits the bald eagle (as per the given statements), the bald eagle doesn\'t need to visit the squirrel.\n\nBut this doesn\'t help with the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nLooking at statement 20 again: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young. The squirrel is red, and the lion visits the squirrel, so the lion is young.\n\nThen, statement 18: If the lion is young, it must visit the bald eagle. But the context doesn\'t say the lion visits the bald eagle, so perhaps the context is missing that information, but according to the rules, the lion should visit the bald eagle.\n\nBut since the context doesn\'t mention it, maybe it\'s not necessary, or perhaps the lion doesn\'t visit the bald eagle, which would mean the lion isn\'t young, contradicting statement 20.\n\nThis is a contradiction, so perhaps the only way out is that the lion is young and does visit the bald eagle, even though it\'s not explicitly stated. So, perhaps the lion visits the bald eagle, which would mean that the bald eagle is visited by the lion, and then by statement 19, the bald eagle visits the squirrel.\n\nBut the context doesn\'t say that the bald eagle visits the squirrel, only that it chases it. So, perhaps the bald eagle doesn\'t visit the squirrel, which would mean that the condition in statement 19 isn\'t met, so the conclusion doesn\'t apply.\n\nBut this is getting too convoluted. Let me try to summarize:.\n\n- Lion visits squirrel (statement 9).\n- Squirrel is red (statement 13).\n- Therefore, lion is young (statement 20).\n- Lion is young, so it must visit the bald eagle (statement 18).\n- But context doesn\'t say lion visits bald eagle, so contradiction.\n- Therefore, perhaps the lion isn\'t young, but that contradicts statement 20.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lion does visit the bald eagle, which is not mentioned, but we have to assume it based on the rules.\n\nIf the lion visits the bald eagle, then by statement 19, the bald eagle visits the squirrel. But the context doesn\'t say that, so perhaps the bald eagle doesn\'t visit the squirrel, which would mean that the condition in statement 19 isn\'t met, so the conclusion doesn\'t apply.\n\nBut this doesn\'t directly tell us about the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nWait, perhaps the bald eagle is young because it visits the squirrel. Let me see.\n\nIf the bald eagle visits the squirrel, then by statement 19, it must have been visited by someone. But does the bald eagle visit the squirrel? The context says the bald eagle chases the squirrel (statement 2), but not that it visits.\n\nAlternatively, if the bald eagle is young, then by statement 18, it would visit itself, which doesn\'t make sense. So, perhaps the bald eagle isn\'t young.\n\nWait, maybe the squirrel is young. Let\'s see.\n\nIf the squirrel is young, then by statement 18, it would visit the bald eagle. But the squirrel doesn\'t visit the bald eagle; it doesn\'t even visit the lion (statement 14). So, perhaps the squirrel isn\'t young.\n\nAlternatively, the squirrel is cold (statement 12), so if something needs the squirrel and it\'s not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle (statement 21). But the squirrel is cold, so the condition isn\'t met.\n\nI\'m getting stuck here. Let me try to approach it differently.\n\nThe question is whether the bald eagle is young. So, I need to see if any statement implies that the bald eagle is young.\n\nLooking at statement 18: If something is young, it visits the bald eagle. So, if the bald eagle is young, then it would visit itself, which is not possible. Therefore, the statement is about others visiting the bald eagle if they are young, not the bald eagle itself being young.\n\nSo, perhaps the bald eagle\'s age isn\'t directly addressed by any statement, making it unknown.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another way. Let\'s see.\n\nIf the lion is young (from statement 20), then it must visit the bald eagle (statement 18). If the lion visits the bald eagle, then by statement 19, the bald eagle visits the squirrel. But the context doesn\'t say that the bald eagle visits the squirrel, only that it chases it. So, perhaps the bald eagle doesn\'t visit the squirrel, which would mean that the condition in statement 19 isn\'t met, so the conclusion doesn\'t apply.\n\nBut this doesn\'t tell us about the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bald eagle is young because it chases things, but that\'s not a given.\n\nWait, maybe the squirrel is young. Let\'s see.\n\nIf the squirrel is young, then by statement 18, it would visit the bald eagle. But the squirrel doesn\'t visit the bald eagle; it doesn\'t even visit the lion (statement 14). So, the squirrel isn\'t young.\n\nAlternatively, the squirrel is red (statement 13), and if something visits the squirrel, it\'s young (statement 20). The lion visits the squirrel, so the lion is young.\n\nBut then, the lion being young must visit the bald eagle (statement 18). If the lion visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle must visit the squirrel (statement 19). But the context doesn\'t say that the bald eagle visits the squirrel, only that it chases it. So, perhaps the bald eagle doesn\'t visit the squirrel, which would mean that the condition in statement 19 isn\'t met, so the conclusion doesn\'t apply.\n\nBut this still doesn\'t tell us about the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nWait, perhaps the bald eagle is cold (statement 3). If something needs the squirrel and it\'s not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle (statement 21). But the squirrel is cold (statement 12), so the condition isn\'t met.\n\nI\'m going in circles here. Let me try to list all the conclusions I can draw:.\n\n- Lion is young (from statements 9, 13, 20).\n- Lion must visit the bald eagle (from statement 18).\n- If lion visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel (statement 19).\n- But the context doesn\'t say the bald eagle visits the squirrel, only that it chases it.\n\nSo, perhaps the context is incomplete, but according to the rules, the lion must visit the bald eagle, which would make the bald eagle visit the squirrel. But since the context doesn\'t mention it, maybe it\'s not necessary, or perhaps the conclusion is that the bald eagle is young because it\'s visited by the lion, but that\'s not directly stated.\n\nWait, no, statement 18 is about something being young leading to visiting the bald eagle, not the other way around. So, the bald eagle\'s age isn\'t determined by who visits it, but by other factors.\n\nI\'m stuck. Maybe the answer is unknown because there\'s no direct statement about the bald eagle\'s age, and the indirect clues lead