      "text": " A) True.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the correct answer was B) False. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let me re-examine. If the bald eagle is rough, it sees the mouse (statement 10). But from statement 8, if someone needs the mouse, the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. So if the mouse is needed by someone, the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. But does the mouse need the bald eagle? No, the lion doesn't need the bald eagle (statement 5). The dog needs the lion (statement 4). The mouse likes the dog but doesn't see it (statement 7). The mouse is liked by the dog (statement 6). The mouse is needed by someone if that someone sees the dog (statement 13). But the mouse doesn't see the dog, so maybe no one sees the dog, meaning no one needs the mouse. Therefore, statement 8 doesn't apply, so the mouse could see the bald eagle or not.\n\nBut from statement 10, the bald eagle sees the mouse. So the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the initial thought was B) False. Maybe I confused myself. Let me try again.\n\nThe bald eagle is rough, so it sees the mouse (statement 10). Therefore, the statement is true. So the correct answer should be A) True.\n\nWait, but the user initially thought the answer was B) False. Maybe I'm missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle because someone needs the mouse. Let's see: If the lion sees the dog, then the lion needs the mouse (statement 13). Then, from statement 8, if the lion needs the mouse, the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. So the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. But the bald eagle seeing the mouse doesn't require the mouse to see the bald eagle. So the bald eagle can see the mouse regardless of whether the mouse sees it.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The bald eagle sees the mouse\" is true.\n\nWait, but the user thought the answer was B) False. Maybe I'm overcomplicating it. Let me try to outline the steps clearly.\n\n1. Bald eagle is kind (statement 1).\n2. All k