      "text": " B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Gary is blue\" is false based on the given information. \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Given Information:**\n   - Anne is green.\n   - Fiona is cold, white, and young.\n   - Gary is green.\n   - Harry is cold and rough.\n   - All young, cold things are white.\n   - If something is cold, it is green.\n   - Young things are white.\n   - White things are blue.\n   - If something is blue, it is red.\n   - If something is green, it is young.\n\n2. **Analyzing Gary:**\n   - Gary is green.\n   - From the rule, if something is green, it is young. So, Gary is young.\n   - Since Gary is young, according to the rule, he should be white.\n   - However, Gary is green, not white. This creates a contradiction because being green implies being young, which in turn implies being white. But Gary is green, not white, which violates the rule that young things are white.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - The contradiction implies that Gary cannot be blue because he is green and not white. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is blue\" is false.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
