ona is quiet, so she must be green. Therefore, Fiona is green, making the statement "Fiona is not green" false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nBut let me go through all the facts to see if there\'s any contradiction or if I\'m missing something.\n\nLooking at Fiona\'s attributes again: rough, quiet, not nice.\n\nFrom fact 12: Quiet → green. So Fiona is green.\n\nFrom fact 13: Round → green. But Fiona\'s roundness isn\'t mentioned, so that doesn\'t directly affect her.\n\nFrom fact 8: Rough and green → round. Since Fiona is rough and green, does that mean she\'s round? Let\'s see. If Fiona is rough and green, then she must be round. But is she round? The facts don\'t say Fiona is round, but according to fact 8, if she\'s rough and green, she must be round. So Fiona would be round.\n\nWait, but does that matter? The question is about her being green, which we\'ve already established from fact 12.\n\nAnother point: All nice things are blue (fact 14). Fiona is not nice, so that doesn\'t apply to her.\n\nAll rough, nice things are blue (fact 15). But Fiona isn\'t nice, so that doesn\'t affect her.\n\nRough, nice things are quiet (fact 16). Again, Fiona isn\'t nice, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Fiona is quiet, so she\'s green</think>.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
