s the cow.\n   - Statement 14: If something visits the cat, it likes the cat. The lion visits the cat (Statement 11), so the lion likes the cat.\n   - Statement 17: If something likes the cat, it chases the squirrel. If the cat likes itself, it would chase the squirrel, but there's no info on the cat chasing the squirrel.\n\n3. **Analyze Self-Liking:**\n   - No statement directly addresses the cat liking itself.\n   - Conditional statements (13, 14, 17) depend on actions (visiting, chasing) not clearly linked to the cat's self-liking.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Insufficient information to confirm or deny the cat's self-liking. Therefore, the statement is unknown.\n\n**Answer:** C) Unknown",
