re\'s no contradiction. Therefore, the lion could still be big without causing a problem.\n\nHmm, this is getting a bit confusing. Let me try to outline the possible chain:.\n\n1. Lion is young (statement 10).\n2. Therefore, lion is big (statement 21).\n3. If lion is big, then lion visits dog (statement 15).\n4. If lion visits dog, then dog eats cow (statement 17).\n5. Cow visits lion (statement 8), which implies cow is alive at the time of the visit.\n\nSo, if the lion visits the dog, the dog eats the cow. But the cow is visiting the lion, so the cow must be alive during that visit. Therefore, the dog eating the cow must happen after the cow visits the lion. So the cow is alive during the visit, then later eaten. That doesn\'t create a contradiction because the visit happens before the eating.\n\nTherefore, the lion can be big without causing a contradiction. So the statement "The lion is big" would be true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that the lion being big leads to the dog eating the cow, which would mean the cow can\'t visit the lion. But if the visit happens before the eating, then it\'s possible. So perhaps the lion is big, and the cow is eaten after visiting the lion.\n\nTherefore, the lion is big, making the statement true.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there\'s any other rule that could affect this. For example, statement 19: If the lion eats the cow, then the lion visits the bald eagle. But the cow is eaten by the bald eagle (statement 1), not the lion. So the lion doesn\'t eat the cow, so statement 19 doesn\'t apply.\n\nAnother point: statement 16 says if someone eats the lion and they are big, then the lion eats the dog. But I don\'t know if anyone eats the lion, so that might not be relevant.\n\nAlso, statement 18: If someone is rough and they eat the cow, then they are young. The bald eagle is rough and eats the cow, so the bald eagle must be young. But the context doesn\'t say that, so maybe that\'s a problem. But since the context doesn\'t mention