t would change color to blue. But does that affect the lion\'s color? Let\'s see.\n\nWait, rule 13 says if someone is red and eats the rabbit, they become blue. So, if the lion (who is red) eats the rabbit, it becomes blue. Then, rule 20 says if someone is blue, they eat the cat. So, if the lion becomes blue by eating the rabbit, then it would eat the cat. But does the lion eat the rabbit? We don\'t know that yet.\n\nAlternatively, if the lion eats the cat, then the cat becomes blue (rule 21). But the cat is red, so if it becomes blue, that\'s a change. But does that affect the lion? Let\'s see.\n\nWait, rule 14: If the lion eats the cat and chases the rabbit, then the cat is kind. So, if the lion eats the cat, and if it chases the rabbit, then the cat is kind. But we don\'t know if the lion chases the rabbit. The lion sees the rabbit, but not necessarily chases it.\n\nThis is getting a bit tangled. Let me try to see if there\'s a chain of reasoning that can lead me to whether the lion eats the tiger.\n\nFirst, the question is about the lion eating the tiger. So, I need to see if any rule implies that the lion does or does not eat the tiger.\n\nLooking through the rules, I don\'t see any that directly link the lion eating the tiger. So, perhaps the answer is unknown. But let me check if any indirect reasoning can lead me to that conclusion.\n\nWait, rule 19 says if someone is nice and they chase the tiger, then they are blue. The lion is nice, so if it chases the tiger, it becomes blue. If the lion becomes blue, then rule 20 says it eats the cat. If the lion eats the cat, then rule 21 says the cat becomes blue. But the cat is red, so that\'s a change.\n\nBut does the lion chase the tiger? The context says the lion sees the tiger (statement 7), but not necessarily chases it. So, unless we can infer that seeing implies chasing, which I don\'t think we can, the lion might not chase the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, the tiger chases the cat and the rabbit (statements 10 and 