entioned is Bob. So, unless Fiona is big, she can\'t be smart. But since we don\'t know, it\'s possible she\'s not big, which would mean she\'s not smart. Alternatively, maybe she is big, but that\'s not stated.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if being big is a requirement for being smart. Fact 10 says smart things are furry, but doesn\'t say anything about being big. However, fact 15 adds that if something is furry and smart, then it\'s big. So, if Fiona is smart, she must be big. But since we don\'t know if she\'s big, we can\'t confirm she\'s smart. Therefore, it\'s possible she\'s not smart.\n\nAlternatively, maybe she is smart, but that would require her to be big, which isn\'t stated. So, without knowing her size, we can\'t be certain. But wait, let\'s see if there\'s any other way to determine her smartness.\n\nFact 8: Big, smart things are kind. So, if Fiona were big and smart, she\'d be kind. But we don\'t know if she\'s kind. However, being kind implies being young (fact 13), which she is. But being young doesn\'t necessarily make her kind. So, she could be kind or not.\n\nWait, but if Fiona were kind, then from fact 13, she is young, which she is. But being kind doesn\'t necessarily require being smart unless she\'s big and smart. So, maybe she\'s kind without being smart, but that\'s not directly supported.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think about the contrapositive of fact 15. If something is not big, then it cannot be both furry and smart. Since Fiona is furry, if she\'s not big, she can\'t be smart. But we don\'t know if she\'s big. So, it\'s possible she\'s not big, which would mean she\'s not smart. But without knowing her size, we can\'t be certain.\n\nWait, but let\'s see if there\'s any other way to determine her smartness. Let\'s look at the facts again.\n\nFact 10: Smart things are furry. So, if Fiona is smart, she must be furry, which she is. But that doesn\'t help us determine if she\'s smart.\n\nFact 15: If furry and smart, then big. So, if she\'s sma